  "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=27$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 27}\\frac{x-27}{\\sqrt{3x}-9} & =\\frac{27-27}{\\sqrt{3(27)}-9}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 27}\\frac{x-27}{\\sqrt{3x}-9} & ={lim}_{x\\to 27}\\frac{(x-27)(\\sqrt{3x}+9)}{(\\sqrt{3x}-9)(\\sqrt{3x}+9)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 27}\\frac{(x-27)(\\sqrt{3x}+9)}{{(\\sqrt{3x})}^{2}-{9}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 27}\\frac{(x-27)(\\sqrt{3x}+9)}{3x-81} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 27}\\frac{(x-27)(\\sqrt{3x}+9)}{3(x-27)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 27}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x}+9}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(27)}+9}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+9}{3} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
